  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $f(x,y)$ is defined when $-1\\le xy\\le 1.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(xy)^{2}}}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ is defined only for \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}{y}^{2} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{2} & <1 \\\\ -1<xy & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is\n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ exists on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le xy\\le 1}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1<xy<1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
